\par\par \b \uc2 Peru's Main Stock Indexes End Down; Sol Strengthens   \b0\par\par\uc2 310 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2007\par 22:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's main stock indexes ended lower Friday, dragged down by weakness in a number of blue-chip shares.   \par \par \uc2 According to preliminary results, the Lima Stock Exchange's general index decreased 157.59 points, or 0.80%, to end at 19,624.61.   \par \par \uc2 The selective blue-chip index ended 378.66 points, or 1.11%, lower at 33,751.89.   \par \par \uc2 Copper miner Sociedad Minera Cerro Verde SAA (CVERDEC1.VL) ended 0.2% lower at $24.15 on weakness in copper prices.   \par \par \uc2 Zinc miner Compania Minera Volcan SAA (VOLCABC1.VL) lost 2.2% to end at 11.35 soles ($3.61) as zinc prices eased.   \par \par \uc2 Financial holding company  \uc2 Credicorp Ltd.\uc2  (BAP) fell 2.4% to end at $60.35.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to purchase $10 million.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help build foreign reserves and to smooth out volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.   \par \par \uc2 Despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sol ended stronger.   \par \par \uc2 The sol ended at a midrate of PEN3.1400 to the U.S. dollar on a spread of PEN3.1390 to PEN3.1410.   \par \par \uc2 The sol ended the previous session at a midrate of 3.1415 soles to the dollar on a spread of PEN3.1410 to PEN3.1420, the strongest level since December 1998.   \par \par \uc2 The sol has gained in recent days as, among other factors, corporations, especially in the mining sector, seek soles for monthly tax payments.   \par \par \uc2 Consulting firm Macroconsult said in a report Friday that foreign banks and local pension funds have been buying soles and selling dollars on the expectation that the U.S. Federal Reserve will move to lower interest rates.   \par \par \uc2 -By Robert Kozak, Dow Jones Newswires; 51-1-221 7050; peru@dowjones.com [ 09-14-07 1639ET ]   \par \par \uc2 1071 1170 1837 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020070914e39e0007e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
