\par\par \b \uc2 Peru's Main Stock Indexes End Mixed; Sol Weakens A Bit   \b0\par\par\uc2 410 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2007\par 23:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's main stock indexes Thursday ended mixed, as some key blue chip shares moved in opposite directions.   \par \par \uc2 Copper miners continued to extend losses, on slightly weaker prices for the red metal.   \par \par \uc2 According to preliminary results, the Lima Stock Exchange's general index fell 14.41 points, or 0.06%, to end at 23,043.56.   \par \par \uc2 The selective blue-chip index ended 30.82 points, or 0.08%, higher at 41,052.80.   \par \par \uc2 On Thursday,  \uc2 Southern Copper Corp.\uc2 (PCU) declined 0.9% to end at $107.00, while copper miner Sociedad Minera Cerro Verde SAA (CVERDEC1.VL) lost 2.2% to end at $27.30.   \par \par \uc2 Going the other way was precious metals miner  \uc2 Compania de Minas Buenaventura\uc2  SAA (BVN), which gained 0.8% to end at 124.50 soles ($39.40) on improved gold prices.   \par \par \uc2 Financial holding company  \uc2 Credicorp Ltd.\uc2  (BAP) rebounded from a decline the previous day to end 3.1% higher at $65.80.   \par \par \uc2 In a report released early Thursday,  \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  Global Markets said that Peru continues to be the best performing regional market, and that year-to-date "MSCI Peru is by far the best performing market globally, rising 84.2%."   \par \par \uc2 It added that, "Surprisingly, however, during the sell-off from July 24-27 this high-beta market fell just 5.2%, compared to the overall regional market decline of 10.2.%"   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the sol Thursday ended slightly weaker at a midrate of 3.1590 soles to the U.S. dollar on a spread of PEN3.1580 to PEN3.1560.   \par \par \uc2 The sol ended the previous session at a midrate of PEN3.1580 to the U.S. dollar on a spread of PEN3.1570 to PEN3.1590.   \par \par \uc2 The Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 Banco de Credito said in a report that higher-than-expected inflationary pressures in Peru have increased the possibility that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could increase its key reference rate and reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy U.S. dollars.   \par \par \uc2 Both those moves would be aimed at allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to comply with its inflation targets.   \par \par \uc2 "The market is betting for more appreciation for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coming months, which is in line with the macroeconomic fundamentals, in particular with the balance of payments," Banco de Credito said.   \par \par \uc2 -By Robert Kozak, Dow Jones Newswires; 51-1-221 7050; peru@dowjones.com [ 08-02-07 1704ET ]   \par \par \uc2 1071 1837   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020070802e382000eo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
