\par\par \b \uc2 Peru's Main Stock Index Rises 1.48% To End At Record   \b0\par\par\uc2 399 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2007\par 22:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's main stock indexes climbed to new records Tuesday on strong interest in mining and banking shares.   \par \par \uc2 According to preliminary results from the Lima Stock Exchange, the stock market's broad general index rose 327.29 points, or 1.48%, to end at 22,493.97.   \par \par \uc2 The selective blue-chip index ended 705.58 points, or 1.86%, higher at 38,695.95.   \par \par \uc2 "The stock market remains very solid. Today, the indexes rose because of strong interest in banking shares and in zinc miner Compania Minera Volcan SAA," said on trader.   \par \par \uc2 "Investors are increasingly interested in the banking sector because of Peru's strong financial system and its solid economy. People's buying power is increasing and when that happens the banking system grows by default," the trader added.   \par \par \uc2 Financial holding company  \uc2 Credicorp Ltd.\uc2  (BAP) ended 3.7% higher to end at $58.40. It owns Peru's largest bank, Banco de Credito.   \par \par \uc2 Compania Minera Volcan SAA (VOLB.VL) gained 6.4% to end at 16.30 soles. ($5.15)   \par \par \uc2 Other mining companies also gained.   \par \par \uc2 Southern Copper Corp.\uc2  (PCU) rose 1.2% to end at $86.00 on stronger copper prices, while copper miner Sociedad Minera Cerro Verde SAA (CVERDEC1.VL) gained 2.1% to end at $29.00.   \par \par \uc2 Precious metals miner  \uc2 Compania de Minas Buenaventura\uc2  SAA (BVN) gained 1.1% to end at PEN109.19.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the sol ended slightly stronger Tuesday, at a midrate of PEN3.1655 to the U.S. dollar on a spread of PEN3.1650 to PEN3.1660.   \par \par \uc2 The sol ended the previous session at a midrate of PEN3.1670 to the U.S. dollar on a spread of PEN3.1660 to PEN3.1680.   \par \par \uc2 The Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to purchase $60 million.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in recent sessions to buy dollars to ease movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and to build foreign reserves.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts say that the sol has appreciated due to strong economic fundamentals, including strong exports.   \par \par \uc2 Flows of dollars into Peru from Peruvians overseas have also risen, while investors are also purchasing soles in order to invest in the stock market, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 -By Robert Kozak and Rebecca Howard, Dow Jones Newswires; 51-1-221 7050 [ 05-08-07 1636ET ]   \par \par \uc2 1071 1837   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020070508e3580007q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
