\par\par \b \uc2 Peru's PM Calls For Probe Into $140 Mln  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Transaction   \b0\par\par\uc2 404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2007\par 21:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's Prime Minister Jorge del Castillo called Tuesday for an investigation into a sale late last week of $140 million by a regional government through a private Peruvian bank.   \par \par \uc2 At a press conference, del Castillo said the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  should have gone through the state-owned Banco de la Nacion, and he raised questions about the propriety of the trade.   \par \par \uc2 "There were serious irregularities. If the national bank pays more, why go to a private bank. Who gained? The region lost. So who gained, and how much?" he said.   \par \par \uc2 The large purchase of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sol, by the regional government of the department of Ancash took place late Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 When the private bank turned around and sold those dollars it had bought, mostly on Friday, that added to appreciation pressures on the sol.   \par \par \uc2 The Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  responded by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to purchase $250 million, to keep the exchange rate from moving too much.   \par \par \uc2 It was the largest single day purchase of dollars ever for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Since then the regional government, which had received a large transfer payment from the central government tied to mining, has been criticized for not getting the best deal when it bought the soles.   \par \par \uc2 At a press conference Tuesday, del Castillo lashed out at the regional government, saying it would have received a better exchange rate had it gone to the state's Banco de la Nacion to make the deal.   \par \par \uc2 Del Castillo said it was likely the comptroller of the nation will look into the movements.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday to mop up the U.S. dollars by paying PEN3.1664 per dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Newspaper El Comercio said in a story Tuesday the government of Ancash received PEN3.1610 per dollar from the private bank it used.   \par \par \uc2 The sol has been on an appreciating trend in recent years, in part as strong exports, especially of minerals, have boosted inflows of cash into Peru.   \par \par \uc2 Various regional governments have been criticized recently for not being able to professionally spend the transfer payments they receive from the central government.   \par \par \uc2 The region of Ancash has received large-scale transfer payments due to presence of some large mines there, including the giant Antamina copper-zinc mine.   \par \par \uc2 -By Robert Kozak, Dow Jones Newswires; 511-221-7050; peru@dowjones.com [ 08-05-07 1952GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070508e35800163\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
