\par\par \b \uc2 Peru's Main Stock Index Rises 3.21% To End At Record   \b0\par\par\uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2007\par 22:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's main stock indexes roared higher to new records Monday, propelled by widespread strength in all sectors.   \par \par \uc2 According to preliminary results from the Lima Stock Exchange, the stock market's broad general index gained 691.23 points, or 3.21%, to end at 22,166.68.   \par \par \uc2 The selective blue-chip index ended 1,206.17 points, or 3.28%, higher at 37,990.37.   \par \par \uc2 Without taking into account Monday's gains, the general index has risen some 67% so far this year, after gaining 168% in 2006.   \par \par \uc2 On Monday, mining companies continued to post strong gains.   \par \par \uc2 Southern Copper Corp.\uc2 (PCU) rose 1.9% to end at $85.00 on stronger copper prices, while copper miner Sociedad Minera Cerro Verde SAA(CVERDEC1.VL) gained 4.8% at end at $28.40.   \par \par \uc2 Precious metals miner  \uc2 Compania de Minas Buenaventura\uc2  SAA (BVN) gained 0.9% to end at 108.00 soles. ($34.10)   \par \par \uc2 Financial holding company  \uc2 Credicorp Ltd\uc2  (BAP) also continued to rise, ending 0.6% higher to end at $56.31, on confidence about economic growth.   \par \par \uc2 Peru's gross domestic product is expected to expand some 8.0% this year, after expanding some 8.0% last year.   \par \par \uc2 "The heaviest traded shares were Credicorp and Volcan. There is a lot of interest in both shares," said one trader.   \par \par \uc2 Zinc miner Compania Minera Volcan SAA (VOLB.VL) gained 2.8% to end at PEN15.32.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the sol ended slightly weaker Monday, at a midrate of PEN3.1670 to the U.S. dollar on a spread of PEN3.1660 to PEN3.1680.   \par \par \uc2 The sol ended the previous session at a midrate of PEN3.1645 to the U.S. dollar on a spread of PEN3.1640 to PEN3.1650.   \par \par \uc2 The Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to purchase $15 million.   \par \par \uc2 That followed a purchase of $250 million on Friday, which was the largest single day purchase ever on the part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in recent sessions to buy dollars to ease movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and to build foreign reserves.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts say that the sol has appreciated due to strong economic fundamentals, including strong exports.   \par \par \uc2 They add that some banks that hold large net U.S. dollar positions have also been selling them.   \par \par \uc2 One local government also recently sold dollars it received in transfer payments tied to resource extraction in the region, market analysts said.   \par \par \uc2 Flows of dollars into Peru from Peruvians overseas have also risen, while investors are also purchasing soles in order to invest in the stock market, analysts add.   \par \par \uc2 -By Robert Kozak and Rebecca Howard, Dow Jones Newswires; 51-1-221 7050 [ 05-07-07 1657ET ]   \par \par \uc2 1071 1837   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020070507e3570006f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
